  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 250 AM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Potential for heavy rain continues across the \nsoutheast today. A Flood Watch remains in effect for south-central \nVA through this morning and Hampton Roads and northeast NC through \nthis evening. \n\nA cold front continues to slowly drop across the Mid-Atlantic and \nwas located across northern counties of the forecast area as of \nearly morning analysis. A mid level trough is positioned over the \neast coast with the axis starting to pivot into central VA. Latest \nradar shows showers with a few rumbles of thunder out ahead of the \nfront filling in across southeast VA and interior NE NC. With PW \nvalues between 2-2.2\", there's plenty of moisture to work with to \nbring heavy rain to the area. The front will eventually drop south \nand bring dry air with it. However, the snail's pace speed of the \nfront likely means wet conditions in the south/southeast through at \nleast this evening. Despite drought conditions, multiple rounds of \nheavy rainfall may cause flooding. The Excessive Rainfall Outlook \nfrom the WPC highlights NE NC and southside Hampton Roads in a \nModerate Risk (lvl 3/4), meaning the potential for numerous \ninstances of flooding. A Slight Risk (lvl 2/4)buffers the Moderate \nand includes the rest of southeast VA. Rain may linger across NE NC \nand extreme SE VA through tonight or even early Friday depending on \nwhere exactly the front settles, but rain rates should be overall \nlower. A Flood Watch remains in effect for southern VA and NE NC. \nThe watch is scheduled to expire for most of the VA counties \n(excluding southside Hampton Roads) at 1pm today, though several \ncounties along the northern and western edge of it will likely get \ntrimmed before then as the heavier rain shifts south. The QPF for \ntoday and tonight is 2-3\" across NE NC and extreme SE VA on top of \nthe 1-2\" that is expected in SE VA before sunrise. \n\nFor the rest of the FA, today will be a teaser for early fall \nconditions with highs in the upper 70s and dewpoints dropping into \nthe low 60s and maybe even upper 50s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below average temperatures are expected tomorrow \nthrough the weekend, before trending back to seasonal for late July \nearly next week. There are daily chances for afternoon storms, but \noverall drier than the current wet pattern.\n\nThe cold front will eventually make it south of the area sometime \ntonight or early Friday, but there will be enough lingering \nmoisture for at least some chc to low-end likely PoPs in far SE \nVA and NE NC Fri and Fri night. The FA will then be positioned \nunder an UL trough over the weekend with onshore flow in place \nat the sfc. This will lead to cooler, below-average \ntemperatures for a few days. Highs on Fri and Sat will be in \nthe upper 70s to around 80. Warming up slightly for Sunday with \nhighs in the low to mid 80s. Near-average temps return early \nnext week. Looking to be overall a dry weekend, but a few \nisolated showers/storms cannot be ruled out during the \nafternoons.",
